// Runtime/ClockRuntime.maxon — the compiler's half of the host-clock family: the three BANDS a clock
// entry point wears, the FILETIME facts the per-target wall-clock chunks are assembled against, and the
// one clock body that is still built as Std IR here.
//
// ⭐ **TWO OF THE THREE ENTRY POINTS ARE MAXON SOURCE, IN `runtime/Clock.maxon`.** `__clock_now_unix_s`
// and `__uptime_ms` are written there against the `__Raw` floor, so the compiler READS them out of the
// tree instead of building them. What stays here is what a source file cannot answer: the band
// predicates, which classify a CALLEE the parser emitted, and `__thread_cpu_ticks`.
//
// ⛔ **WHY `__thread_cpu_ticks` IS THE ONE THAT CANNOT MOVE.** Its green-thread arm reads the running
// green thread's clock offset (`GtRuntime.emitLoadCurrentGtCpuOffset`), which goes through the
// current-GT pointer — an op with no `__Raw` row. The `__Raw` table is the floor a runtime source file
// stands on, so an entry needing an op the table does not carry has no spelling there.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THAT IS NOT A CLOCK.** The other two measure WALL time, so a duration
// taken with either counts every OTHER process on the box: a compiler phase timed on a busy machine
// reports the machine. This one advances only while the CALLING THREAD is scheduled, which is what makes
// `PhaseProbe`'s per-phase cost survive a loaded host — `docs/optimization-log.md`'s `## CPU` table is
// what it feeds.
//
// ⚠ **ITS UNIT IS PLATFORM-DEFINED AND THERE IS NO HONEST CONVERSION**: TSC ticks on Windows
// (`QueryThreadCycleTime`), nanoseconds under POSIX (`clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID)`).
// `QueryPerformanceFrequency` is the PERFORMANCE COUNTER's rate and not the TSC's, so a normalization
// here would be a guess wearing a unit's name. The entry point says `ticks`; callers compare RATIOS,
// which are unit-free, or absolutes within one platform.
//
// ⭐ **THREE BANDS RATHER THAN ONE, AND THE SPLIT IS ABOUT COST.** All three are refused on the same
// lanes, so they do not divide by "may this target run it". They reach three DIFFERENT Win32 imports,
// and the optional trailing import band is gated per producer: one shared bit would make a program that
// reads the calendar import `QueryThreadCycleTime`, and a program that measures its own cost import
// `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime`.

// The prefix every wall-clock runtime entry point shares — the "does this program read the CALENDAR?" test.
let ClockRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__clock_"

export let WallClockNowUnixSecondsName = b"__clock_now_unix_s"

// The `__thread_` band — the "does this program ask how much CPU TIME its own thread has used?" test, and
// the second thing `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` reads out of this file for E3104. A PREFIX
// rather than a name compare for `isProcessRuntimeCallee`'s reason: an entry point added to this band later
// is gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ⚠ **DELIBERATELY NOT `__cpu_`.** That band is `isCpuCountRuntimeCallee`, and it drives `usesCpuCount` —
// the sole producer of `GetActiveProcessorCount` and of the optional trailing import band it turns on.
// Filing this entry there would make every program that times a phase import an API it never calls, and
// install `__cpu_count`'s body beside it. The two questions are unrelated besides: one asks the MACHINE how
// many processors exist, the other asks the host how long THIS thread has run.
let ThreadCpuRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__thread_"

export let ThreadCpuTicksName = b"__thread_cpu_ticks"

// ⭐⭐ **THE UPTIME COUNTER — MILLISECONDS SINCE THE MACHINE BOOTED (`GetTickCount64`), AND IT IS A CLOCK
// OF ITS OWN RATHER THAN A SPELLING OF ONE ABOVE.** The DebugStream wire format is measured in it: the
// producer stamps every ring entry with `osTickCountMs() - start_timestamp`, so a consumer that seeds
// that header field with any OTHER counter turns each delta into a number with no meaning. QPC-ms
// (`Clock.nowMs()` → `__gt_now_ns` scaled) LEADS the tick, which makes the producer's subtraction
// underflow; the calendar is not monotonic at all. Only the same counter closes it.
let UptimeRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__uptime_"

export let UptimeMsName = b"__uptime_ms"

// The out-param scratch is a FRAME slot, not a `.data` word, and that is a safety rule rather than
// tidiness: two Ms do not need a yield point to be inside one function at once, so two threads writing
// one shared word would hand each other the other's reading — here, another thread's cycle count.
let ClockScratchBytes = 8 as StackSize
let ClockScratchSlot = 0 as SpillSlot

// A FILETIME counts 100-NANOSECOND intervals since 1601-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. Two facts turn that into Unix
// seconds, and both are named rather than spelled inline at the arithmetic:
//   • 10,000,000 ticks per second (100 ns each);
//   • 11,644,473,600 seconds from the 1601 epoch to the 1970 one — 116444736000000000 ticks, which fits an
//     i64 with three orders of magnitude to spare.
//
// ⚠ **THEY ARE READ BY THE PER-TARGET WALL-CLOCK CHUNKS, NOT BY ANY BUILDER HERE.** A POSIX host answers
// nanoseconds, and its hand-assembled `osReadWallClock` converts to FILETIME ticks so that one piece of
// arithmetic — `runtime/Clock.maxon`'s — serves every lane. `runtime/` names nothing in `maxon-bin/`, so
// that file states the same two facts; the tier boundary is what forbids sharing them.
export let FileTimeTicksPerSecond = 10000000
export let FileTimeTicksFrom1601To1970 = 116444736000000000

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE READ THE WALL CLOCK?** The `__clock_`-only test, the exact twin of
// `isGtRuntimeCallee`. Deliberately NARROW: unlike a `__gt_*` call it implies no scheduler and no heap —
// `__clock_now_unix_s` is a leaf that calls one host op and divides, so a calendar-reading program
// carries neither the allocator nor the leak gate.
export function isClockRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ClockRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isClockRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE ASK HOW MUCH CPU TIME THIS THREAD HAS USED?** The `__thread_`-only test, the exact
// twin of the wall clock's above and narrow in the same way: `__thread_cpu_ticks` is a leaf that calls one
// Win32 import and loads a word, so a phase-timing program carries neither the allocator nor the scheduler.
// (With a scheduler already installed it also reads the running green thread's clock offset.)
export function isThreadCpuRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ThreadCpuRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isThreadCpuRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE ASK HOW LONG THE MACHINE HAS BEEN UP?** The `__uptime_`-only test, and narrow in its
// neighbours' way: `__uptime_ms` is a leaf that calls one host primitive and returns it, so a program that
// measures against the DebugStream wire clock carries neither the allocator nor the scheduler.
export function isUptimeRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: UptimeRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isUptimeRuntimeCallee'

// Install the host-clock runtime into the lowered Std module. ONE entry point is left to install, and it
// keeps its own bit for the reason the three bands exist: the import it costs is not the import its two
// source-tier siblings cost.
//
// Appended AFTER the pipeline (like every other runtime install), and already-lowered Std so it still
// flows through the whole backend. What runs over that appended band is stated once, in
// `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s install block.
export function installClockRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesThreadCpuTicks 'threadCpu'
		buildThreadCpuTicks(module, greenThreads: usage.usesGt)
	end 'threadCpu'
end 'installClockRuntime'

// ⭐ **`GetCurrentThread()`'s PSEUDO-HANDLE, `(HANDLE)-2`** — a compile-time constant that always names the
// calling thread, not a handle to open or close, so naming this thread costs neither an import nor a call.
// It is stated HERE because `StdOp.osThreadCpuTicks` takes its subject as an operand: the one party that
// means "this thread" is this builder, and the backends see only a value.
//
// ⚠ Spelled as an arithmetic negation rather than as a transcribed two's-complement pattern, which is
// `InvalidHandleValue`'s idiom: a hand-written 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE is sixteen digits nothing checks.
let CurrentThreadPseudoHandle = 0 - 2

// ⭐⭐ **`__thread_cpu_ticks() -> i64`** — the CPU time this thread has consumed, in the host's own unit.
//
// `osThreadCpuTicks` writes `QueryThreadCycleTime`'s tick count into a `.data` scratch word and this loads
// it straight back — `__clock_now_unix_s`'s shape with the arithmetic removed, because there is none to do.
// That absence is the contract rather than an omission: the two platforms answer in units that do not
// convert (TSC ticks vs nanoseconds), so any scaling written here would be the dishonest normalization the
// header refuses. What a caller may do with two readings is SUBTRACT them, and a difference is meaningful
// in either unit.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IN A GREEN-THREAD PROGRAM IT IS THE CALLING GREEN THREAD's CLOCK** — the counter plus the thread's own
// offset (`GtRuntime.GtOffCpuOffset`), which its parks and yields keep continuous. Every caller there runs on
// a green thread, and a spawned one can resume on another OS thread after any park; the bare counter would
// then run backwards across it.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT IS NOT CLAMPED AND MUST NOT BE.** `__cpu_count` clamps because a processor count is a
// DIVISOR for its callers, so a 0 escaping would be a division by zero; a tick count is only ever
// subtracted from a later one. A thread that has just started legitimately reads a very small number, and a
// floor imposed here would make the first bracket of a phase report a cost it did not have.
function buildThreadCpuTicks(module StdModule, greenThreads bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ThreadCpuTicksName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The out-param lives in THIS CALL's frame, not in `.data` — see `ClockScratchSlot`.
	func.stackSize = ClockScratchBytes
	let ticksAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(ticksAddr, slot: ClockScratchSlot))

	// The subject is an operand rather than an isel immediate, so the pseudo-handle naming "this thread" is
	// stated at the caller that means it (`StdOp.osThreadCpuTicks`).
	let currentThread = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: CurrentThreadPseudoHandle as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osThreadCpuTicks(currentThread, outPtrId: ticksAddr))
	let ticks = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: ticksAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)

	if not greenThreads 'theOsThreadIsTheCaller'
		module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(ticks))
	end 'theOsThreadIsTheCaller' else 'aGreenThreadIsTheCaller'
		module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(emitBin(module, block: entry, a: ticks, b: emitLoadCurrentGtCpuOffset(module, block: entry, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)))
	end 'aGreenThreadIsTheCaller'

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildThreadCpuTicks'
